From 49e9cabb7ba37e59d3cb2f28f31c7e12d21a5c7e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 13:03:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/12] S3-1: PS1 resident bytes back to 0, an option change
 cannot burst the pass, one edge removal

The first cut added 64 B to the PS1's resident code (the rule until PS1-RAM lands is none): sim_tick's loop
ends on its own (the shipped clocks earn at most 15 slots a tick: static assert; the loop count and the
clamp after it are gone), update_resident_ex keeps its old two arguments (the pass line SIM_PASS_MIN 8 and
the fast-forward line SIM_MIN_ELAPSED 15 are one compare on force_decide), minute_step reads
OPT_EVENT_RATE once, the activity is stored without the compare, and sim_edge_remove (a copy of town.c's
remove_edge) is gone: both are rel_remove_at now. PS1: text 2,818,396 -> 2,818,400 B, the EXE still ends
0x801efe60 (65,952 B below the stack top with the FMV), as on 698f4eee.

One thing the loop count did: it bounded a tick when the Options page changes the day length (the credit
left in the old clock's units could be 96 slots in the new one). A tick now pays at most SIM_BATCH slots
(test_sim_pass: day length change). rel_remove_at closes the gap with a halfword move on the GBA like
rel_get_or_add opens it (the edge pass's removals and the full graph's evictions); test_town checks both
against a reference table.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
